# Key ceremony: repository root (YubiKey A) and release key (YubiKey B)

> **Status: OWNER DRAFT, revision 1, 2026-11-02.** It generates the two offline keys V1 needs. It is based on
> `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md` §6–§8 (custody, YubiKey configuration, ceremony) and the first rehearsal
> (`2026-11-01-rehearsal-1.md`). The key type comes from `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §15.4: a v4 ECDSA P-256 key. That
> rehearsal showed Tails' GnuPG produces signatures the verifier accepts on the git path. Per-release signing
> (custody draft §9) and incident handling (§10) are separate and not covered here.
> Items marked **AUTHOR** are the author's choices. Mark them on paper, never in the repository.

## Why two keys, two sessions, separate backups

- **Repository root (A)** signs the key-assertion commits (each window's service keys) and the end-of-issuance
  statement. It is the git channel's authority (`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md` §1).
- **Release key (B)** signs verifier releases, which carry the trust file that names A. Whoever holds B decides which
  repository keys a verifier trusts. So B must not be capturable together with A, the DNS credential or the AWS
  account (A10.4.2; runbook :121–129).
- So each key gets **its own session, its own passphrase and its own backup sticks**. A thief who finds one
  backup location, or learns one passphrase, gets one key, not both.

## What you need (per key)

| Item | Count | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Tails boot stick | 1 (shared) | Verified image (**AUTHOR**: version, and where the checksum was verified). Boot only; nothing written to it. |
| YubiKey | 1 | A for the root, B for the release key. Label it before starting. C and D stay spares. |
| Backup sticks | 2 | **New**, never inserted in the NUC. Labelled `ROOT-BK1`/`ROOT-BK2`, or `REL-BK1`/`REL-BK2`. |
| Out stick | 1 | New. Carries only public files: the public key and the proof signature (steps 6 and 9). It goes to the NUC afterwards and never returns to a signing session. The revocation certificate stays on the backup sticks only. |
| Paper | 2 sheets | One for the fingerprint (public). One for the passphrase (secret), stored apart from the backups. |
| Passphrase method | — | **AUTHOR.** Recommended: diceware, 7 words, from physical dice, generated in the room. |

Total new sticks for both keys: **6** (4 backup, 2 out). The boot stick is not new.

## Owner decisions in this procedure (2026-11-02)

- **Primary custody: hardware mode** (custody draft §6): the key lives on the YubiKey; the backups are the only
  other copies.
- **Backups: the secret-key export (protected by the key passphrase) on a LUKS-encrypted stick** formatted by Tails'
  *Disks* utility with its defaults (LUKS2). Two layers: LUKS passphrase and key passphrase. **The same passphrase
  may serve both layers of one key's backups**, because a thief with the stick and that passphrase gets the key
  either way. It must differ between A and B.
- **KDF on the card: off.** It hashes the PIN on the host so it does not cross USB in the clear. The only host is an
  offline Tails session, so it adds a step and buys nothing in our model.
- **No expiry on either key.** Validity is set by the trust configuration's signing interval (profile §6.3) and by
  release lineage, not by the OpenPGP expiry field.
- **Touch fixed, signature PIN forced**, as rehearsed (`uif 1 permanent`, `forcesig`).

## Session 1: generate and back up (repeat in full for each key, on separate occasions)

Before booting: network cable out, Wi-Fi off. At the Tails Welcome Screen, set an administration password
(needed by *Disks*), then start Tails. Open a terminal.

**1. Check the card.** Insert the labelled YubiKey (A or B) only.
```
gpg --card-status
```
Confirm: Manufacturer Yubico, no keys, PIN retries `3 0 3`, `UIF setting` present. Write the serial number on the
label and on the public sheet.

**2. Set the PINs** (new values, never used elsewhere; **AUTHOR** keeps them with the passphrase sheet):
```
gpg --card-edit
admin
passwd
```
`3` (admin PIN, default `12345678`), then `1` (user PIN, default `123456`), then `q`, `q`.

**3. Set the slot to P-256:**
```
gpg --card-edit
admin
key-attr
```
Signature key: `2` (ECC), then `3` (NIST P-256). Leave encryption and authentication as they are. Then `q`.

**4. Generate the key in memory:**
```
gpg --expert --full-generate-key
```
`10` (ECC, sign only), `3` (NIST P-256), expiry `0`.
Name: `Tessera repository root` (A) or `Tessera release key` (B). Email: `tessera@wamason.com`.
Enter the passphrase. Then:
```
gpg --list-keys --with-fingerprint
```
Write the full fingerprint on the public sheet. Read it back once.

**5. Make the two backups.** For each backup stick in turn (only one inserted at a time):
1. Open *Disks* → select the stick → menu → *Format Disk* (GPT) → `+` → create a partition, type *Internal disk
   for use with Linux systems only (Ext4)*, tick *Password protect volume (LUKS)*, enter the passphrase. Name it
   `ROOT-BK1` (etc.).
2. Unlock and mount it in *Files*, then:
```
B=/media/amnesia/ROOT-BK1          # the name shown in Files
FPR=<the fingerprint>
gpg --export-secret-keys --armor $FPR > $B/secret.asc
gpg --export --armor $FPR > $B/public.asc
cp ~/.gnupg/openpgp-revocs.d/$FPR.rev $B/revocation.rev
sync && ls -l $B
```
3. Eject in *Files*. Remove the stick.

**6. Export the public key to the out stick** (plain stick; nothing secret ever goes on it):
```
gpg --export --armor $FPR > /media/amnesia/<OUT>/<root|release>.pub.asc
sha256sum /media/amnesia/<OUT>/*.pub.asc
```
Write the SHA-256 on the public sheet. Eject the out stick.

**7. Move the key to the card.** Only after both backups exist:
```
gpg --edit-key $FPR
keytocard
```
`1` (signature key), give the passphrase and the admin PIN, then `save`.

**8. Set the card policy:**
```
gpg --card-edit
admin
forcesig
uif 1 permanent
q
```
Both are silent on success.

**9. Prove the card works:**
```
echo "tessera <root|release> ceremony $(date -u +%FT%TZ)" > ~/proof.txt
gpg --digest-algo SHA256 --armor --detach-sign ~/proof.txt
```
It asks for the **user** PIN. Then **the YubiKey blinks: touch it within about 15 seconds** (GnuPG does not say so;
a timeout means no touch). Then:
```
gpg --verify ~/proof.txt.asc ~/proof.txt
gpg --card-status
```
Card status should show the signature key with your fingerprint, `Signature PIN: forced`, `UIF … Sign=on` (or
permanent), signature counter 1. Copy `proof.txt` and `proof.txt.asc` to the out stick if you want the owner to
check them with the verifier's code, as in the rehearsal.

**10. Shut down Tails.** Remove the YubiKey. The in-memory keyring and exports are gone.

## Session 2: restore test (each backup, each in a fresh boot)

A backup that has never been restored is a claim, not a copy. For each backup stick, separately:

1. Fresh Tails boot, network off. Insert only that backup stick (not the YubiKey). Unlock it in *Files*.
2. Restore and check:
```
gpg --import /media/amnesia/ROOT-BK1/secret.asc
gpg --list-secret-keys --with-fingerprint
```
The fingerprint must match the paper exactly.
3. Sign and verify from the restored copy:
```
echo restore-test > ~/r.txt && gpg --digest-algo SHA256 --armor --detach-sign ~/r.txt && gpg --verify ~/r.txt.asc ~/r.txt
```
4. Shut down. Record pass or fail on the public sheet.

## After both keys

- **Storage (AUTHOR):** each key's two backups in two different places, and A's and B's backups not together.
  Tell the owner only that this is done, never where. Passphrase sheets apart from the backups.
- **On the NUC (owner):** the out stick is mounted read-only. The owner checks each public key with the verifier's
  code (82-byte v4 P-256 body), and checks the proof signatures, and writes the dated custody record (fingerprints,
  serials, custody mode, this revision and its hash, rehearsal link, "backups in two places: yes"). No locations,
  no secrets.
- **Independent fingerprint check (AUTHOR):** on a device that is not the NUC (a phone works), compute or read the
  public key's fingerprint from the out stick and compare it with the paper. This is the custody draft's "public-key
  bootstrap" step 1–2.
- The root's public key then goes into the trust configuration's `repository_keys`; the release key's fingerprint
  is published through the channels the author names (custody draft §8).

## Time

The rehearsal steps took seconds each. Estimate per key: about 45–60 minutes, mostly formatting and mounting the
backup sticks, plus about 10 minutes per restore test. This is an estimate until the first real session measures it.
# Key ceremony rehearsal 1 — repository root, throwaway key (2026-11-01/02)

Performed by the author on a Tails live session, network off; directed by the owner. Purpose: learn whether a
P-256 key generated on Tails and used from a YubiKey produces signatures the V1 verifier accepts on the git path,
and time the steps, before the real ceremony (YubiKey A, repository root; YubiKey B, release key).
Procedure basis: `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md` §6–§8; key type from `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §15.4.

## Hardware
- YubiKey C (spare; rehearsal only), serial 39975816. Reader `1050:0407` (YubiKey 5, OTP+FIDO+CCID); OpenPGP
  application 3.4 (firmware ≥ 5.2.3, so P-256 capable). Factory state on arrival: no keys, PIN retries 3/0/3,
  signature counter 0, KDF off, UIF off, key attributes rsa2048 ×3. (Photo: author, public fields only.)

## Throwaway key
- `6F3D071A8761DF5187C578DABB89158A63C8EA8F`, nistp256 (algo 19), created 2026-11-02 (epoch 1793577872),
  uid "Tessera repository root REHEARSAL <tessera@wamason.com>", capabilities scSC. Discarded with the session.

## Result (owner, on the NUC, from the transfer stick mounted read-only, noexec)
- GnuPG (isolated keyring): good signature. Packet: v4, sigclass 0x00, digest SHA-256 (8), hashed {33 issuer fpr,
  2 creation time}, unhashed {16 issuer key id} — the layout profile §8 expects.
- Verifier code (`tessera_verify.git_channel.dearmor`, `codec.sig_policy`, `codec.public_key(·, 'gpg')`,
  `codec.signature_verifies`): armor, rule O and policy PASS; the 82-byte v4 key body accepted; signature over
  `t.txt` verifies; one byte changed: does not verify.

## Lessons for the real procedure
- Add `key-attr` (signature slot → ECC, NIST P-256) before `keytocard`; factory slots are RSA 2048.
- The exported public key starts with an old-format packet header with a one-byte length (`0x98`).
- The author declined to photograph the secret key even in rehearsal: keep that rule explicit for the real one.
- The transfer stick touched the NUC; it is "out only" from now on and never returns to a signing session.
- Author's notes: `forcesig` and `uif 1 permanent` both returned silently (accepted). Signing asked for the
  **user** PIN. The first attempt failed with a **timeout**: GnuPG does not say "touch the key", the YubiKey only
  blinks. The second attempt, with a touch, succeeded. So touch is enforced; the procedure must say "when the
  YubiKey blinks, touch it within about 15 seconds". Each step took a few seconds.
- YubiKey C: factory reset (step 8) still pending as of 2026-11-02; to be done before the session shuts down.
- The author on not photographing the secret key: "the walk-through is when I want to instill the discipline,
  so I don't screw it up when the real thing is ready." The rehearsal is practice of the habits, not only a test of
  the tools.

