     1	# Key ceremony: repository root (YubiKey A) and release key (YubiKey B)
     2	
     3	> **Status: OWNER DRAFT, revision 1, 2026-11-02.** It generates the two offline keys V1 needs. It is based on
     4	> `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md` §6–§8 (custody, YubiKey configuration, ceremony) and the first rehearsal
     5	> (`2026-11-01-rehearsal-1.md`). The key type comes from `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §15.4: a v4 ECDSA P-256 key. That
     6	> rehearsal showed Tails' GnuPG produces signatures the verifier accepts on the git path. Per-release signing
     7	> (custody draft §9) and incident handling (§10) are separate and not covered here.
     8	> Items marked **AUTHOR** are the author's choices. Mark them on paper, never in the repository.
     9	
    10	## Why two keys, two sessions, separate backups
    11	
    12	- **Repository root (A)** signs the key-assertion commits (each window's service keys) and the end-of-issuance
    13	  statement. It is the git channel's authority (`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md` §1).
    14	- **Release key (B)** signs verifier releases, which carry the trust file that names A. Whoever holds B decides which
    15	  repository keys a verifier trusts. So B must not be capturable together with A, the DNS credential or the AWS
    16	  account (A10.4.2; runbook :121–129).
    17	- So each key gets **its own session, its own passphrase and its own backup sticks**. A thief who finds one
    18	  backup location, or learns one passphrase, gets one key, not both.
    19	
    20	## What you need (per key)
    21	
    22	| Item | Count | Notes |
    23	|---|---|---|
    24	| Tails boot stick | 1 (shared) | Verified image (**AUTHOR**: version, and where the checksum was verified). Boot only; nothing written to it. |
    25	| YubiKey | 1 | A for the root, B for the release key. Label it before starting. C and D stay spares. |
    26	| Backup sticks | 2 | **New**, never inserted in the NUC. Labelled `ROOT-BK1`/`ROOT-BK2`, or `REL-BK1`/`REL-BK2`. |
    27	| Out stick | 1 | New. Carries only public files: the public key and the proof signature (steps 6 and 9). It goes to the NUC afterwards and never returns to a signing session. The revocation certificate stays on the backup sticks only. |
    28	| Paper | 2 sheets | One for the fingerprint (public). One for the passphrase (secret), stored apart from the backups. |
    29	| Passphrase method | — | **AUTHOR.** Recommended: diceware, 7 words, from physical dice, generated in the room. |
    30	
    31	Total new sticks for both keys: **6** (4 backup, 2 out). The boot stick is not new.
    32	
    33	## Owner decisions in this procedure (2026-11-02)
    34	
    35	- **Primary custody: hardware mode** (custody draft §6): the key lives on the YubiKey; the backups are the only
    36	  other copies.
    37	- **Backups: the secret-key export (protected by the key passphrase) on a LUKS-encrypted stick** formatted by Tails'
    38	  *Disks* utility with its defaults (LUKS2). Two layers: LUKS passphrase and key passphrase. **The same passphrase
    39	  may serve both layers of one key's backups**, because a thief with the stick and that passphrase gets the key
    40	  either way. It must differ between A and B.
    41	- **KDF on the card: off.** It hashes the PIN on the host so it does not cross USB in the clear. The only host is an
    42	  offline Tails session, so it adds a step and buys nothing in our model.
    43	- **No expiry on either key.** Validity is set by the trust configuration's signing interval (profile §6.3) and by
    44	  release lineage, not by the OpenPGP expiry field.
    45	- **Touch fixed, signature PIN forced**, as rehearsed (`uif 1 permanent`, `forcesig`).
    46	
    47	## Session 1: generate and back up (repeat in full for each key, on separate occasions)
    48	
    49	Before booting: network cable out, Wi-Fi off. At the Tails Welcome Screen, set an administration password
    50	(needed by *Disks*), then start Tails. Open a terminal.
    51	
    52	**1. Check the card.** Insert the labelled YubiKey (A or B) only.
    53	```
    54	gpg --card-status
    55	```
    56	Confirm: Manufacturer Yubico, no keys, PIN retries `3 0 3`, `UIF setting` present. Write the serial number on the
    57	label and on the public sheet.
    58	
    59	**2. Set the PINs** (new values, never used elsewhere; **AUTHOR** keeps them with the passphrase sheet):
    60	```
    61	gpg --card-edit
    62	admin
    63	passwd
    64	```
    65	`3` (admin PIN, default `12345678`), then `1` (user PIN, default `123456`), then `q`, `q`.
    66	
    67	**3. Set the slot to P-256:**
    68	```
    69	gpg --card-edit
    70	admin
